.TH "URL.HREF" "3JS" "March 28, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
URL.href \- URL: href property
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]href\f[B]\f[R] property of the \f[CR]URL\f[R] interface
is a string containing the whole URL.
.SH VALUE
A string.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Full URL with path
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] url = \f[B]new\f[R] URL(
  \(dqhttps://developer.mozilla.org/en\-US/docs/Web/API/URL/href\(dq,
);
console.log(url.href); \f[I]// https://developer.mozilla.org/en\-US/docs/Web/API/URL/href\f[R]
.EE
.SS Origin\-only URL
For \c
.UR https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc3986#section-1.2.3
hierarchical schemes
.UE \c
\ such as \f[CR]https:\f[R], the pathname must always be non\-empty.
When the URL has no path (for example, only an origin), the URL parser
sets the pathname to \f[CR]\(dq/\(dq\f[R], so \f[CR]href\f[R] includes a
trailing slash.
See the \f[CR]pathname\f[R] property for more details.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] url = \f[B]new\f[R] URL(\(dqhttps://developer.mozilla.org\(dq);
console.log(url.href); \f[I]// https://developer.mozilla.org/\f[R]
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.IP \(bu 2
The \f[CR]URL\f[R] interface it belongs to.
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]URL.pathname\f[R]
